## Why Asked
Tests understanding of graph traversal fundamentals, algorithm selection, and space-time tradeoffs in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
**BFS (Breadth-First Search)**: Uses queue, explores neighbors level-by-level. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(V) worst case (all nodes in queue). Guarantees shortest path in unweighted graphs.

**DFS (Depth-First Search)**: Uses stack or recursion, explores as deep as possible before backtracking. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(h) where h is maximum depth (recursion stack), O(V) worst case for skewed graphs.

## When to Use Each
**BFS**: Shortest path routing, social network friend suggestions, web crawlers finding closest pages, level-order tree traversals.

**DFS**: Topological sorting, cycle detection, maze solving, finding connected components, backtracking problems like Sudoku or N-Queens.

## Code Example
```javascript
// BFS - finds shortest path
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const distances = { [start]: 0 };

  while (queue.length) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        visited.add(neighbor);
        distances[neighbor] = distances[node] + 1;
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return distances;
}

// DFS - recursive implementation
function dfs(graph, node, visited = new Set(), result = []) {
  if (visited.has(node)) return result;

  visited.add(node);
  result.push(node);

  for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
    dfs(graph, neighbor, visited, result);
  }

  return result;
}

// DFS - iterative implementation
function dfsIterative(graph, start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (visited.has(node)) continue;

    visited.add(node);
    result.push(node);

    // Add neighbors to stack (reverse for consistent order)
    for (let i = graph[node].length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const neighbor = graph[node][i];
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        stack.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Memory Considerations
BFS can consume significant memory for wide graphs (queue holds all nodes at current level). DFS is memory-efficient for deep, narrow graphs but may cause stack overflow for very deep recursion. Choose based on graph structure and problem requirements.

## Concept Overview
An LRU (Least Recently Used) cache efficiently manages memory by evicting the least accessed items when capacity is reached. This implementation leverages a HashMap for fast key lookups and a DoublyLinkedList for maintaining access order.

## Implementation Details
- **HashMap**: Maps keys to Node references, enabling O(1) lookup operations
- **DoublyLinkedList**: Maintains access order with head representing most recently used items and tail representing least recently used items
- **Thread Safety**: ReentrantReadWriteLock provides optimal concurrency - multiple threads can read simultaneously while writes are mutually exclusive
- **Eviction Policy**: When capacity is exceeded, the tail node (least recently used) is removed
- **Operations**: Both get() and put() operations maintain O(1) time complexity

## Code Example
```java
public class LRUCache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> cache;
    private final DoublyLinkedList<K, V> order;
    private final ReentrantReadWriteLock lock;
    private final int capacity;

    public LRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new HashMap<>();
        this.order = new DoublyLinkedList<>();
        this.lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> node = cache.get(key);
            if (node != null) {
                // Upgrade to write lock for reordering
                lock.readLock().unlock();
                lock.writeLock().lock();
                order.moveToHead(node);
                return node.value;
            }
            return null;
        } finally {
            if (lock.isWriteLocked()) {
                lock.writeLock().unlock();
            } else {
                lock.readLock().unlock();
            }
        }
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try {
            Node<K, V> existing = cache.get(key);
            if (existing != null) {
                existing.value = value;
                order.moveToHead(existing);
                return;
            }

            Node<K, V> newNode = new Node<>(key, value);
            cache.put(key, newNode);
            order.addToHead(newNode);

            if (cache.size() > capacity) {
                Node<K, V> tail = order.removeTail();
                cache.remove(tail.key);
            }
        } finally {
            lock.writeLock().unlock();
        }
    }
}```

